{
  "overall_assessment": "The revised deck mostly teaches the intended story. As a fresh technical audience member, I came away with the right ownership boundary, but the remaining risks are: DSA/offload still feels like the deck destination in a few places, the exact Tonic API change lands later than it could, and Prost resume correctness is more asserted than demonstrated.",
  "learned_story": [
    "Slides 1-2: This is not just adding `async`; encode can now suspend, so the important question is where encode state lives while suspended.",
    "Slide 2: The old sync path is easy because `encode` borrows `EncodeBuf` only during the call, writes complete message-body bytes, then Tonic can frame them.",
    "Slide 3: A borrowed async shape breaks because the Tonic body driver must keep polling after the call returns, so a future borrowing caller stack state is the wrong unit.",
    "Slides 4-5: `Pending` makes readiness visible but does not solve ownership; anything needed after suspension has to move into the encode operation or another long-lived owner.",
    "Slide 6: The final Tonic boundary is an owned `T::Encode: Future<Output = Result<EncodeBuffer, Error>> + Send + 'static`; Tonic stores it in `in_flight` and gets the `EncodeBuffer` back on `Ready`.",
    "Slide 7: Async encode fills message-body bytes only; Tonic still owns gRPC framing: header reservation, compression, size checks, length/header write, and yielding bytes.",
    "Slides 8-9: Prost, not Tonic, owns protobuf-level continuation: field/index progress and the tag/length/payload phase so a resumed encode does not corrupt the wire format.",
    "Slide 10: DSA/IAX matters because hardware may hold raw source/destination addresses while the future is pending, making owned stable state a safety requirement.",
    "Slides 11-12: Performance is a later measurement question; the reusable rule is that stored/polled operations must own the state needed after suspension."
  ],
  "divergence_from_original_goal": [
    "Slides 1, 10-11, and the footer still make DSA feel slightly too central. The footer phrase `async Tonic encode → DSA-backed Prost encode` reads like the destination is DSA-backed Prost, even though the contract says DSA/IAX is only the motivating offload case.",
    "The exact contract phrase `one owned T::Encode future per in-flight message` is implied but not made memorable. Slide 6 shows `in_flight: Option<T::Encode>`, but the audience may remember “owned future” without the per-message/in-flight constraint.",
    "The stack-local borrowed-buffer-to-owned-buffer transition is taught, but the deck separates failure (slide 3), ownership migration (slide 5), and final API (slide 6). A fresh learner must assemble the concrete before/after conversion themselves.",
    "Prost’s responsibility is correct, but slides 8-9 teach it as an abstract state inventory. The goal’s `field/tag/length/payload resume state` would land harder with one concrete suspended field example.",
    "The deck succeeds at subordinating measurement on slide 11, but slide 11 still introduces enough cost-model detail that a listener could briefly shift from “what changed architecturally” to “when does DSA win?”"
  ],
  "top_5_slide_edits": [
    {
      "rank": 1,
      "slides": "All slides footer + slide 1",
      "edit": "Change the running footer from `async Tonic encode → DSA-backed Prost encode` to something like `borrowed sync encode → owned async Tonic encode`. On slide 1, label DSA/IAX as `motivating Pending source`, not part of the main path.",
      "why": "This is the highest-impact fix for keeping async Tonic encode as the center and DSA as consequence."
    },
    {
      "rank": 2,
      "slides": "Slides 3 and 6",
      "edit": "Add a single explicit before/after strip: `sync: encode(..., &mut EncodeBuf) returns complete bytes` → `async: encode(..., EncodeBuffer) returns owned T::Encode` → `driver stores one future per in-flight message` → `Ready returns EncodeBuffer`.",
      "why": "It turns the core contract into one memorable conversion instead of three separate ideas."
    },
    {
      "rank": 3,
      "slides": "Slide 5",
      "edit": "Replace the scattered inventory chips with one nested box labeled `the T::Encode object Tonic stores`, containing `message`, `EncodeBuffer`, `Prost PollEncodeState`, `DSA/IAX copy state`, `descriptor/completion`, and `drop/cancel state`.",
      "why": "The current slide teaches “state moves somewhere”; the intended story needs “state moves into the owned future Tonic stores.”"
    },
    {
      "rank": 4,
      "slides": "Slides 8-9",
      "edit": "Add one concrete Prost resume example: `field N length-delimited: tag written → length written → payload DSA copy Pending → next poll resumes payload only`. Keep the failure list smaller or attach failures to this example.",
      "why": "This makes `exact field/tag/length/payload resume state` observable instead of abstract."
    },
    {
      "rank": 5,
      "slides": "Slide 7",
      "edit": "Reduce the responsibilities list to three grouped steps: `reserve 5-byte header`, `poll owned encode future and regain buffer`, `finalize compression/size/header/yield`. Keep the pipeline and buffer-shape diagram; remove the small code/text block.",
      "why": "Slide 7 currently over-explains framing and competes with the key boundary: encoder owns body bytes, Tonic owns frame finalization."
    }
  ],
  "comprehension_blockers": [
    {
      "slide": "3",
      "issue": "Strong teaching point, but the bottom failure diagram has small labels and the code card takes a lot of attention. The phrase `caller-borrowed state cannot become Tonic’s long-lived in-flight encode operation` should be visually as dominant as the `T::Encode: Send + 'static` line.",
      "suggested_fix": "Make the red diagram larger and explicitly label `borrowed future is not Send + 'static / not storeable by Tonic`."
    },
    {
      "slide": "5",
      "issue": "The phrase `or a longer-lived owner` weakens the intended answer, and the inventory chips are spread out enough that I do not immediately see one owned future object.",
      "suggested_fix": "Use the nested `T::Encode` ownership box described above; if keeping the rule, follow it with `In this design, that owner is the encode future.`"
    },
    {
      "slide": "7",
      "issue": "Highest density blocker. Pipeline, body boundary, code/text block, responsibility bullets, buffer shape, and warning all compete. The slide is correct but slow to parse.",
      "suggested_fix": "Keep only the pipeline, a compact buffer shape, and three grouped responsibilities."
    },
    {
      "slide": "9",
      "issue": "The tag → length → payload sequence is clear, but the resume inventory and corruption list are abstract for a first-time listener.",
      "suggested_fix": "Replace some inventory chips with one worked `Pending` checkpoint example and attach one or two corruption outcomes to the wrong checkpoint."
    },
    {
      "slide": "10",
      "issue": "The long thesis plus hardware path plus five invariants can make DSA/DMA safety feel like a new main topic.",
      "suggested_fix": "Compress to one cause/effect: `Pending offload means raw addresses outlive the call`, then three invariants: source valid, destination stable, completion/drop drains work."
    }
  ]
}

