{
  "overall_assessment": "The deck mostly teaches the intended ownership story. As a fresh reader, I would leave with the right headline, but the exact per-message lifecycle is still distributed across several slides; the highest-impact revision is to make Slide 6 the single canonical timeline of buffer-in, stored future, Pending, Ready(buffer), frame-finalization.",
  "learned_story": [
    "Sync Tonic encode used a short-lived borrowed destination: encode borrowed the buffer, wrote complete message-body bytes, returned, and only then did Tonic frame the bytes.",
    "Async/offload changes the problem because an encode can return Pending before the message body is complete, so state has to survive past the call boundary.",
    "A tempting `async fn encode(..., &mut EncodeBuf)` shape is wrong because it creates caller-borrowed suspended state; the body driver needs a storeable `Send + 'static` unit.",
    "Polling readiness alone was not enough: `Pending` says work is incomplete, but it does not decide who owns the item, buffer, progress, offload state, cancellation, or completion.",
    "The final Tonic boundary is: move an `EncodeBuffer` into `encode`, get back an owned `T::Encode` future, store it as the one in-flight message operation, poll it, and regain the `EncodeBuffer` on `Ready`.",
    "The stored `T::Encode` object owns the message, buffer, Prost poll state, DSA/IAX copy state, descriptor/completion, and drop/cancel state needed to resume safely.",
    "Tonic still owns the gRPC frame: it reserves the 5-byte header, polls the encode future for body bytes, then finalizes compression, size checks, flags, length, and yielding.",
    "Prost owns exact protobuf resume points; DSA/IAX explains why Pending and stable ownership matter physically, while performance measurement is a downstream consequence."
  ],
  "divergences_from_goal": [
    "Mostly aligned: the deck clearly centers the owned `T::Encode` future and Prost resume-state split.",
    "The concrete conversion in the goal is spread across Slides 3, 5, and 6. I learn the ownership rule, but I have to assemble the exact lifecycle myself instead of seeing one unavoidable timeline.",
    "Slides 4 and 8 risk a polling ambiguity: “poll encode moved down” can sound like Tonic no longer polls, even though the intended story is that Tonic polls the coarse `T::Encode` future while Prost owns fine-grained protobuf poll state.",
    "Slides 1, 10, and 11 still give DSA/offload/performance substantial visual area. The text says DSA is not the main story, but a first-time viewer could over-weight hardware as the deck’s destination unless the speaker reinforces otherwise.",
    "Slides 2/3 use `EncodeBuf` while Slides 6/7 use `EncodeBuffer`. If this is not an intentional old/new type distinction, it creates a distracting apparent rename; if it is intentional, the deck should say so."
  ],
  "highest_impact_slide_edits": [
    {
      "slides": "6",
      "edit": "Make this the canonical lifecycle slide. Replace the three equal code cards with one large sequence: `item + EncodeBuffer` → `encode(item, dst)` moves buffer → `in_flight = Some(T::Encode { ... })` → `poll = Pending` → `poll = Ready(Ok(EncodeBuffer))` → `Tonic finalizes frame`. Keep the type alias/signature as small evidence attached to the relevant steps."
    },
    {
      "slides": "5",
      "edit": "Turn the bottom inventory into a large cutaway of the actual `T::Encode` object labeled “lives across Pending.” Put `message`, `EncodeBuffer`, `Prost PollEncodeState`, `DSA/IAX copy state`, `descriptor/completion`, and `drop/cancel` inside one capsule. Shrink the “before: split state” side to a warning, not an equal competitor."
    },
    {
      "slides": "8-9",
      "edit": "Make Slide 8 the layer map and Slide 9 a zoom into the `Prost PollEncodeState` box using the same color/label. Add the clarifying sentence on Slide 8: “Tonic polls the future; Prost records the byte-level continuation inside it.” On Slide 9, mark tag/length as “already emitted” and payload as “pending at offset N” so resume exactness is concrete."
    },
    {
      "slides": "1",
      "edit": "Demote DSA/IAX from an equal top card to a smaller “why Pending exists” chip. Make the old→new API conversion and final thesis the dominant first read. Add a short label such as “motivating example, not destination” near DSA if space allows."
    },
    {
      "slides": "4",
      "edit": "Compress this into a bridge slide titled “Why `poll_encode` Was Not Enough.” Keep only: readiness answered; ownership unresolved; therefore owned future cutover. Remove or shrink project-history wording like “middle design” so the audience does not detour away from the current Tonic boundary."
    }
  ],
  "comprehension_blockers": [
    {
      "slide": "1",
      "issue": "Three top cards have similar weight, so DSA/IAX can feel co-equal with the target architecture.",
      "fix": "Make the conversion strip and thesis visually primary; make DSA a smaller motivating cause of Pending."
    },
    {
      "slide": "5",
      "issue": "The most important object, `T::Encode`, is split between an after card and a low, wide inventory. The eye sees many small labels rather than one owned suspended operation.",
      "fix": "Use one large `T::Encode` container with all resume-needed state inside it."
    },
    {
      "slide": "6",
      "issue": "This is the key boundary slide, but the code cards compete with the lifecycle; `Ready returns EncodeBuffer` is visually less important than it should be.",
      "fix": "Show the complete per-message timeline as the main object and subordinate code to annotations."
    },
    {
      "slide": "8",
      "issue": "“Poll encode moved down, not away” is good but can be misread as Tonic no longer polling anything.",
      "fix": "Explicitly distinguish “Tonic polls the coarse future” from “Prost owns inner field-level poll state.”"
    },
    {
      "slide": "9",
      "issue": "The worked checkpoint is strong, but the correct/wrong lists are dense and slightly detached from the byte sequence.",
      "fix": "Attach the errors directly under the sequence positions: duplicate tag under tag, duplicate length under length, wrong offset under payload."
    },
    {
      "slide": "10",
      "issue": "Hardware safety is clear, but it is visually strong enough to pull attention toward DSA as the main topic.",
      "fix": "Add a small header/footer cue like “consequence of the owned-future boundary” and keep the owned `T::Encode` label prominent in the cause/effect path."
    },
    {
      "slide": "11",
      "issue": "The balance graphic, measurement questions, and dark reminder create three focal points; performance may feel like a new chapter just before the conclusion.",
      "fix": "Reduce to one takeaway band plus two columns: removable cost vs new costs. Keep measurement questions smaller or move them to speaker notes/appendix."
    }
  ]
}

